import LegalLayout from "../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Data Processing Addendum"
  description="GDPR roles and obligations for Merkleye. In most deployments we are not a processor of your monitoring data at all."
  updated="[EFFECTIVE DATE]"
>
  <p>
    This addendum forms part of the
    <a href="/legal/license/">Commercial License Agreement</a> between
    <span class="tbd">[ENTITY LEGAL NAME]</span> and Customer, and applies where
    we process personal data on Customer&rsquo;s behalf.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Scope — start here</h2>
  <p>
    Merkleye is deployed on Customer&rsquo;s own infrastructure. Certificate
    data, monitored domains, findings, and notifications are processed entirely
    within Customer&rsquo;s environment and are never transmitted to us.
    <strong>In respect of that data we are not a processor, not a controller,
    and not a recipient.</strong> There is no transfer to address, no
    subprocessor chain, and no deletion obligation, because we never hold it.
  </p>
  <p>This addendum therefore covers only two narrow categories:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>Our role</th><th>Data</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Account and billing records</td>
        <td>Controller</td>
        <td>Contact name, work email, organisation, billing details, licence records</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Support diagnostics, if enabled</td>
        <td>Processor</td>
        <td>Installation identifier, licence identifier, version, platform, operational counters</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Support correspondence</td>
        <td>Processor</td>
        <td>Whatever Customer chooses to send us in a ticket</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>2. Roles and instructions</h2>
  <p>
    Where we act as processor, Customer is the controller. We process personal
    data only on Customer&rsquo;s documented instructions, which this addendum
    and the agreement constitute, and we will tell Customer if an instruction
    appears to breach applicable data protection law.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Subject matter and duration</h2>
  <p>
    Subject matter: providing contracted support. Duration: the term, plus the
    retention periods in the <a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
    Categories of data subject: Customer&rsquo;s personnel who administer the
    deployment or contact support.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Support diagnostics specifically</h2>
  <p>
    Diagnostics are <strong>disabled by default</strong> and require an explicit
    configuration change by Customer. A local-only mode writes the payload to
    Customer&rsquo;s own disk and transmits nothing, which is the recommended
    setting for air-gapped and high-assurance deployments.
  </p>
  <p>
    The payload contains counts, versions, booleans, and enumerated error
    classes. It <strong>never</strong> contains domain names in any form
    including hashed, certificates, subject alternative names, findings,
    scores, configuration contents, credentials, or free-text error messages.
    Customer can print the exact payload before enabling transmission. We
    retain it for 13 months.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Confidentiality and security</h2>
  <p>
    Personnel with access are bound by confidentiality obligations. We maintain
    technical and organisational measures appropriate to the risk, including:
    encryption in transit and at rest; least-privilege access with multi-factor
    authentication enforced by hardware keys; separated production and
    development environments; logging and monitoring of administrative access;
    documented change management; and periodic review.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most significant measure is architectural: we do not receive
    Customer&rsquo;s monitoring data, so it cannot be exposed by a compromise of
    our systems.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Subprocessors</h2>
  <p>
    Customer authorises the subprocessors listed at
    <a href="/legal/subprocessors/">/legal/subprocessors</a>. We will give at
    least <span class="tbd">[30]</span> days&rsquo; notice before adding or
    replacing one; Customer may object on reasonable data-protection grounds,
    and if we cannot resolve the objection Customer may terminate the affected
    service without penalty. We remain liable for our subprocessors&rsquo; acts.
  </p>

  <h2>7. International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Where personal data is transferred out of the UK or EEA, the transfer is
    made under the EU Standard Contractual Clauses (Module Two, controller to
    processor) or the UK International Data Transfer Addendum, which are
    incorporated by reference. Docking clause: optional. Governing law and
    forum follow Clause 17 and 18 as
    <span class="tbd">[MEMBER STATE]</span>.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Data subject requests</h2>
  <p>
    Taking into account the nature of the processing, we will assist Customer
    in responding to data subject requests. Given the narrow data we hold, that
    assistance is normally a lookup and an export. If a data subject contacts
    us directly about data we process for Customer, we will refer them to
    Customer rather than respond substantively.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Personal data breach</h2>
  <p>
    We will notify Customer without undue delay and in any event within
    <span class="tbd">[48]</span> hours of becoming aware of a personal data
    breach affecting Customer&rsquo;s personal data, with the nature of the
    breach, likely consequences, and measures taken. Notice is not an admission
    of fault.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Deletion and return</h2>
  <p>
    On termination we delete or return personal data processed on
    Customer&rsquo;s behalf within <span class="tbd">[90]</span> days, except
    where retention is required by law. Backups age out on their normal cycle.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Audit</h2>
  <p>
    We will make available the information reasonably necessary to demonstrate
    compliance, and respond to a security questionnaire once a year. Given that
    we hold no monitoring data, on-site audit is not offered; where a
    Customer&rsquo;s regulator requires one, the parties will agree a
    proportionate alternative in writing.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Order of precedence</h2>
  <p>
    In case of conflict: the Standard Contractual Clauses, then this addendum,
    then the <a href="/legal/license/">Commercial License Agreement</a>, then
    the <a href="/legal/terms/">Terms of Service</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
